{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":353376,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"aae4be49-8038-491e-af0c-5944152e4e46","total_cost_usd":1.0475785,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32648,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":27776,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32648,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":27776,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32648,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32648},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":18105,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.018205,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":27776,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":32648,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.0293735,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"researcher\",\n  \"planet\": {\n    \"radius_m\": 250000,\n    \"surface_gravity_ms2\": 4.0,\n    \"atmosphere_top_m\": 78000,\n    \"scale_height_m\": 6000,\n    \"sea_level_density_kgm3\": 1.0\n  },\n  \"bands\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"suborbital\",\n      \"altitude_min_m\": 85000,\n      \"altitude_max_m\": 120000,\n      \"orbital_speed_ms\": 842,\n      \"period_s\": 2630\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"low\",\n      \"altitude_min_m\": 140000,\n      \"altitude_max_m\": 200000,\n      \"orbital_speed_ms\": 772,\n      \"period_s\": 3421\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"high\",\n      \"altitude_min_m\": 230000,\n      \"altitude_max_m\": 320000,\n      \"orbital_speed_ms\": 690,\n      \"period_s\": 4781\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reentry\": {\n    \"heating_onset_speed_ms\": 400,\n    \"plasma_onset_speed_ms\": 650,\n    \"heating_velocity_exponent\": 3.0,\n    \"drag_coefficient_hull\": 0.5,\n    \"drag_coefficient_shield\": 1.5,\n    \"reference_area_m2\": 3.5\n  },\n  \"derivation\": [\n    \"Planet first, everything else after. R = 250000 m sits in the charter's 160-320 km window; g = 4.0 m/s^2 was chosen with it so the two produce round numbers: mu = g*R^2 = 4.0 * (2.5e5)^2 = 4.0 * 6.25e10 = 2.5e11 m^3/s^2, and the grazing circular speed is sqrt(g*R) = sqrt(4.0*250000) = sqrt(1.0e6) = 1000 m/s exactly. Treat 1000 m/s as this planet's natural velocity unit; every speed below is quoted as a fraction of it.\",\n    \"Escape speed = sqrt(2*g*R) = sqrt(2*4.0*250000) = sqrt(2.0e6) = 1414 m/s. This bounds everything in the reentry model: no trajectory that starts from a bound orbit can enter faster than 1414 m/s, and in practice entries land between 860 and 960 m/s (step 7).\",\n    \"Scale height comes from hydrostatic equilibrium, H = R_specific * T / g. Working backwards from the air column I need: H = 6000 m at g = 4.0 requires R_specific * T = 6000 * 4.0 = 24000 J/kg, e.g. a CO2-dominated atmosphere (R_specific = 189 J/kg/K) at T = 127 K. Earth air (287 J/kg/K) at 250 K under this gravity would give H = 287*250/4.0 = 17900 m and a sensible atmosphere ~230 km deep, taller than the planet's radius and deeper than the lowest band. A cold, heavy gas is what keeps the air column thin enough to sit under the bands; that is a derived requirement, not a flavour choice.\",\n    \"Atmosphere model is rho = rho0 * exp(-h/H) with rho0 = 1.0 kg/m^3, truncated at atmosphere_top. At h = 78000 m = 13 H, rho = 1.0 * exp(-13) = 2.26e-6 kg/m^3. Drag deceleration there for a full staged ship (beta = 533, step 8) at 1000 m/s is 0.5 * 2.26e-6 * 1.0e6 / 533 = 2.1e-3 m/s^2, about 5 m/s of decay per 2630 s orbit, so cutting density to zero at that height costs almost nothing physically. Earth reaches comparable negligibility at ~14.6 e-folds (1.225 kg/m^3 at sea level to ~5.6e-7 at the 100 km Karman line); 13 e-folds is the same convention, tightened slightly to keep the sky compact.\",\n    \"Band speed, substitution shown for suborbital. Band midpoint h = 102500 m, so r = R + h = 250000 + 102500 = 352500 m. v = sqrt(mu/r) = sqrt(2.5e11 / 352500) = sqrt(709220) = 842 m/s. Period T = 2*pi*r/v = 2*pi*352500 / 842 = 2214911 / 842 = 2630 s = 43.8 minutes.\",\n    \"Same substitution for the other two. Low: r = 250000 + 170000 = 420000, v = sqrt(2.5e11/420000) = sqrt(595238) = 772 m/s, T = 2*pi*420000/772 = 3421 s = 57 minutes. High: r = 250000 + 275000 = 525000, v = sqrt(2.5e11/525000) = sqrt(476190) = 690 m/s, T = 2*pi*525000/690 = 4781 s = 80 minutes. Orbital periods therefore run 44-80 minutes and the on-rails time warp promised in the charter is mandatory, not optional. This is the price of an air column deep enough to aerobrake through.\",\n    \"Entry-interface speeds, vis-viva v = sqrt(mu*(2/r - 1/a)) evaluated at the atmosphere top r = 328000 m, for a deorbit that drops periapsis to r_p = 290000 m (40 km altitude). From suborbital: a = (352500+290000)/2 = 321250, v = sqrt(2.5e11*(6.098e-6 - 3.113e-6)) = sqrt(746200) = 864 m/s. From low: a = 355000, v = sqrt(2.5e11*(6.098e-6 - 2.817e-6)) = 906 m/s. From high: a = 407500, v = sqrt(2.5e11*(6.098e-6 - 2.454e-6)) = 954 m/s.\",\n    \"Ballistic coefficient of the staged ship, beta = m / (Cd * A). Assumed masses (mine, for this check only, to be priced downstream): staged reentry mass 1400 kg empty, ~2800 kg with a full hold, per GDD 2.3.5's claim that a full hold roughly doubles ship mass. Reference area A = 3.5 m^2 is the frontal disc of a ~2.1 m diameter ship. With Cd_shield = 1.5, Cd*A = 5.25 m^2, so beta = 1400/5.25 = 267 kg/m^2 empty and 2800/5.25 = 533 kg/m^2 full. Apollo's command module runs ~350-390 kg/m^2, so both figures sit in or just under the real capsule band and nothing is below the 100 kg/m^2 floor where first contact with air kills the whole orbit.\",\n    \"Ballistic coefficient of the unstaged hull. Nose-forward slender body, Cd = 0.5 on the same 3.5 m^2 reference area gives Cd*A = 1.75 m^2. With the stage and a full tank still attached, mass is ~2600 kg empty-hold to ~4000 kg loaded, so beta = 1486 to 2286 kg/m^2. Staging therefore improves braking authority by 2286/533 = 4.3x: three times the drag area and a ~30% mass drop at once. That ratio is what gives the staging decision physics behind it.\",\n    \"Aerobrake pass sanity check, using the standard single-pass approximation dv = rho_p * v * sqrt(2*pi*H*r_p) / (2*beta). At a 40 km periapsis: rho = 1.0*exp(-40000/6000) = exp(-6.667) = 1.27e-3 kg/m^3, sqrt(2*pi*6000*290000) = sqrt(1.093e10) = 104562 m, so dv = 1.27e-3 * 900 * 104562 / (2*533) = 119796/1066 = 112 m/s per pass, at a peak deceleration of 0.5*1.27e-3*900^2/533 = 0.97 m/s^2 (0.25 g). At a 55 km periapsis with the unstaged hull (beta = 2286): rho = exp(-9.167) = 1.05e-4, dv = 2.2 m/s per pass - a genuine feather. At 25 km: rho = exp(-4.167) = 1.55e-2 and the same formula returns 1334 m/s, more speed than the ship has, i.e. a single committed entry, peak deceleration 0.5*1.55e-2*810000/533 = 11.8 m/s^2 (3.0 g). The corridor reads cleanly: 50-60 km feathers, 35-45 km brakes, below 30 km commits.\",\n    \"Mass effect on pass count, which GDD 2.3.5 and 2.3.7 both depend on: dv per pass scales as 1/beta, and beta scales with mass. Filling the hold takes beta from 267 to 533, which halves dv per pass from 224 to 112 m/s at a 40 km periapsis. A full haul from the top band therefore needs about twice the passes an empty ship needs from the same orbit, with no other parameter changed. The claim is load-bearing and it survives the arithmetic.\",\n    \"Convective heating, Sutton-Graves: q = 1.7415e-4 * sqrt(rho/R_nose) * v^3.04, with R_nose = 1.0 m for the blunt shield. At the 40 km braking pass (rho = 1.27e-3, v = 900): q = 1.7415e-4 * 0.0356 * 7.29e8 = 4.5e3 W/m^2 = 4.5 kW/m^2. At a 25 km committed pass (rho = 1.55e-2): q = 1.7415e-4 * 0.1246 * 7.29e8 = 1.58e4 W/m^2 = 15.8 kW/m^2. The 3.5x spread between a braking pass and a committed one is the room the ablation curve has to work in. Note the absolute scale: Earth entry runs 0.5-5 MW/m^2, roughly 100x hotter, because v^3 at 8.7x lower speed dominates the higher density.\",\n    \"Heating onset 400 m/s = 0.40 of the velocity unit. Justification by the cube law: at 400 m/s the flux is (400/900)^3 = 8.8% of a committed pass's peak, which is where 'heat is building' stops being a lie. This also matches GDD 2.3.1's 'roughly half of orbital speed' read at band altitude: 0.5 * 842 = 421 m/s. Exponent used is 3.0 (Sutton-Graves is 3.04 on velocity and 0.5 on density; the density exponent matters as much as the velocity one and should be carried into the config).\",\n    \"Plasma onset 650 m/s = 0.65 of the unit, above heating onset as the charter requires, and low enough that every entry in step 7 (864-954 m/s) arrives already in plasma and clears it during descent, which is what makes it usable as the chute gate. It is a cue, not ionisation: stagnation temperature T0 = T + v^2/(2*cp) with cp ~ 750 J/kg/K gives 127 + 650^2/1500 = 409 K at plasma onset and only 733 K at the fastest entry. See notes - real air on this planet never glows.\"\n  ],\n  \"sources\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"mu = g*R^2, circular speed v = sqrt(mu/r), period T = 2*pi*r/v, and vis-viva v = sqrt(mu*(2/r - 1/a)) for the entry-speed derivation\",\n      \"reference\": \"Newtonian gravitation and the two-body vis-viva relation, standard astrodynamics\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Convective stagnation-point heating q = 1.7415e-4 * sqrt(rho/R_nose) * v^3.04, i.e. velocity cubed and density to the half power\",\n      \"reference\": \"Sutton-Graves correlation (NASA TR R-376, 1971), constant quoted for SI air; cited from knowledge, no network access\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Exponential atmosphere rho = rho0*exp(-h/H) with H = R_specific*T/g, and the H = 6000 m / R_specific*T = 24000 arithmetic\",\n      \"reference\": \"Hydrostatic equilibrium for an isothermal ideal gas; US Standard Atmosphere 1976 as the Earth calibration (H ~ 7-8 km in the troposphere)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"13 scale heights is a fair place to truncate the atmosphere - Earth is ~14.6 e-folds from sea level to the Karman line\",\n      \"reference\": \"Earth sea-level density 1.225 kg/m^3 vs ~5.6e-7 kg/m^3 at 100 km; ln(1.225/5.6e-7) = 14.6\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Ballistic coefficient of 267-533 kg/m^2 for the staged ship is in the real capsule range\",\n      \"reference\": \"Apollo command module: ~5560 kg, 3.9 m base diameter (A ~ 12 m^2), hypersonic Cd ~ 1.2-1.35, giving beta ~ 350-390 kg/m^2\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Cd 1.5 for the blunt exposed shield and 0.5 for the slender nose-forward hull\",\n      \"reference\": \"Blunt-capsule hypersonic drag coefficients run 1.2-1.6 (Apollo, Soyuz, Mercury); slender launch-vehicle axial Cd runs 0.3-0.5 supersonic\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Single-pass aerobrake delta-v approximation dv = rho_p*v*sqrt(2*pi*H*r_p)/(2*beta)\",\n      \"reference\": \"Standard aerobraking/orbital-decay analysis via the effective atmospheric path length sqrt(2*pi*H*r) (King-Hele-style decay theory); cited from knowledge\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"A small game planet with a truncated exponential atmosphere is established practice, not an invention of this document\",\n      \"reference\": \"Kerbal Space Program's Kerbin: R = 600 km, atmosphere cut at 70 km, scale height ~5.6 km - about 12.5 e-folds, the same convention used here\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Speed of sound a = sqrt(gamma*R_specific*T) = 176 m/s and stagnation temperature T0 = T + v^2/(2*cp), used for the plasma and chute notes\",\n      \"reference\": \"Compressible-flow relations for an ideal gas; gamma = 1.29 and cp ~ 750 J/kg/K for CO2 at low temperature\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"notes\": [\n    \"PLASMA IS NOT PHYSICAL AT THIS SCALE. Air ionises enough to glow at stagnation temperatures around 4000 K, which on this planet needs v = sqrt(2*cp*(4000-127)) = sqrt(2*750*3873) = 2410 m/s. Escape velocity is 1414 m/s, so no bound trajectory can ever produce real plasma here. plasma_onset_speed_ms = 650 is a gameplay cue chosen to sit above heating onset and to clear during every entry, exactly as GDD 2.3.1 needs. It is the largest single place where the game's constraints beat the physics, and the crew should treat it as an authored threshold, not a derived one.\",\n    \"The chute gate is nominally supersonic. Sound speed in this cold heavy gas is sqrt(1.29*189*127) = 176 m/s, so plasma clearing at 650 m/s is Mach 3.7. Real parachutes deploy below Mach 1.5-2. On Earth the ratio works because orbital speed is Mach 26 and there is a long subsonic tail after blackout ends; here orbital speed is only Mach 5.7, so the tail is missing. Recommend the Coder gate the chute on dynamic pressure q = 0.5*rho*v^2 in addition to the plasma flag, or accept supersonic deploy as convention.\",\n    \"Band-to-band entry speeds only spread 864 -> 906 -> 954 m/s, so velocity-cubed heating gives the high band just (954/864)^3 = 1.35x the peak flux of the suborbital band. GDD 2.3.7's 'each band up is disproportionately hotter' will NOT be carried by entry speed alone on a planet this small - every band sits between 61% and 68% of escape velocity, and that range cannot be widened without periods far past 80 minutes. The differentiation has to come from the two levers the GDD already names: heavier, denser hauls up high (which doubles pass count via beta, per derivation step 11) and band-gated ablation rates (GDD 4.5 risk 2's stated mitigation). Flagging this early because it looks like a tuning problem and is actually a scaling one.\",\n    \"Absolute heat flux is ~100x below Earth reentry (4.5-16 kW/m^2 here vs 0.5-5 MW/m^2 there). A real ablator would barely char at these fluxes. The shield's ablation-per-pass coefficients therefore cannot be derived from real ablator enthalpy of ablation - they must be authored in game units so that the fixed thermal-cycling toll plus the heat-dependent term put the optimum at 2-4 passes. That is the Playtester's job; I am flagging that there is no physical number for it to anchor to.\",\n    \"The planet cannot exist. g = 4.0 m/s^2 at R = 250 km implies bulk density 3g/(4*pi*G*R) = 3*4.0/(4*pi*6.674e-11*250000) = 57000 kg/m^3, roughly three times osmium. Every small-planet game has this problem (Kerbin implies ~58000 kg/m^3 too). Stated for the record; nothing downstream depends on the planet being constructible.\",\n    \"Charter arithmetic discrepancy, resolved in favour of the explicit metres. '1/40 to 1/20 of Earth's diameter' works out to 318-637 km, not the '160 km to 320 km' the same sentence gives; 160-320 km is 1/40 to 1/20 of Earth's RADIUS. I used the explicit metre range and picked 250 km. If the fraction was the intended constraint, the planet should be ~400 km and every speed here rises by ~26% at the same gravity.\",\n    \"Time warp is now on the record as a hard requirement: periods are 2630 s (suborbital), 3421 s (low) and 4781 s (high). A three-pass aerobrake descent from the high band spans roughly 4 orbits, about 5.3 hours of in-world time. At 100x warp that is 3 minutes of play; at 10x it is half an hour and unplayable. Suggest the warp ceiling be at least 100x and that it be available while coasting between aerobrake passes specifically.\",\n    \"Atmospheric composition is under-determined and slightly awkward. H = 6000 m at g = 4.0 needs R_specific*T = 24000, and the combination I quote (CO2 at 127 K) is below CO2's sublimation temperature at the ~0.3 bar surface pressure that rho0 = 1.0 kg/m^3 implies - it would snow out. A heavier gas at a warmer temperature (molar mass ~66 g/mol at ~185 K) satisfies the same product without freezing. Nothing downstream reads composition, only H and rho0, so I left the gas unspecified in the output; flagging that the tidy version of the story does not quite close.\",\n    \"Ship masses in the ballistic-coefficient check are my assumptions, not derived: 1400 kg staged and empty, 2800 kg staged with a full hold, 2600-4000 kg unstaged. They came from GDD 2.3.5's 'a full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass' plus a guess at absolute scale. If the crew prices the ship differently, beta moves proportionally and derivation steps 8-11 must be recomputed. beta = 267 kg/m^2 for the empty staged capsule is the lowest figure in the set and the one to watch - it is above the 100 kg/m^2 danger line by a comfortable margin but below Apollo's ~350.\",\n    \"The unstaged hull's Cd = 0.5 assumes the ship stays nose-forward through its shallow braking passes. Broadside, the reference area is several times larger and the shallow-pass numbers in derivation step 10 stop holding. Whether attitude affects drag area is a Coder decision; if it does not, the hull Cd should be raised to an attitude-averaged value.\",\n    \"Mr Armstrong's satellite sits above the high band, so it wants an orbit around 360 km altitude (r = 610000, v = sqrt(2.5e11/610000) = 640 m/s, period 5988 s = 100 minutes). I did not put it in bands because the charter asks for exactly three, but the return from there is the hottest entry in the game: with periapsis at 290000, entry-interface speed is 985 m/s, and at the satellite's stated mass the ship's beta roughly doubles again, so the descent is both faster and harder to slow down.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":267724,"ttft_stream_ms":1385,"time_to_request_ms":228,"type":"result","duration_ms":351751,"uuid":"cede250e-7ec6-4459-a6f1-7b79ffb554b5"}
